{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"Invariant|invariant|Everything is|Derive|Host Boundaries|Interpretation Creates|No hidden|callbacks|shared mutable|graphs\\\" docs/design/datom.world.md | head -120; sed -n '1,90p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"Invariant|invariant|Everything is|Derive|Host Boundaries|Interpretation Creates|No hidden|callbacks|shared mutable|graphs\\\" docs/design/datom.world.md | head -120; sed -n '1,90p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md\"","aggregated_output":"2:description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n9:Everything is data.\n16:1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n17:2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n19:4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n21:## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n25:- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n26:- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n28:- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n64:### Host Boundaries\n134:**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n167:2. Identify invariants.\n# yin.vm de Bruijn VM\n\nStatus: design; not implemented\n\nThis document specifies a second executable path for `yin.vm`. The existing\npath lowers named Universal AST datoms to `:yin.code/*` and executes that\nimage. This design defines a sibling VM for a derived linear image. An\noptional lossless de Bruijn source view may be added separately. The projection\nremains a separate identity and deduplication artifact.\n\n## 1. Architecture and invariants\n\nThree artifacts are associated with one named AST. The named datoms are the\nbijective source representation. The projection is the alpha-canonical,\nlossy identity representation. The executable de Bruijn image is a third,\nexecutable, derived representation and is not itself invertible.\n\nArchitecture A means lowering from projected records. Architecture B, the\nrecommended architecture, lowers from named datoms and is not the same as A.\nLowering projected records cannot promise named execution semantics because\nprojection deliberately canonicalizes scalar spelling, preserves free names\nonly in canonical form, removes binder names and provenance, and drops\nfront-end `:yin/tail?`. Restricting the input to already-canonical programs,\nweakening continuation guarantees, and abandoning the fingerprint as a cache\nkey would make that path sound but would make it a poor executable artifact.\n\nThe optional lossless source view contains:\n\n- positional bound references `[frame-depth position]`;\n- exact literal and name spellings from the named datoms;\n- an explicit diagnostic name table for binders;\n- `:yin/tail?` on every node;\n- provenance and source entity references, with tempids normalized as described\n  below.\n\nIts invertibility invariant is:\n\n    decode-db(encode-db(named-datoms))\n      = named-datoms modulo the declared normalization below\n\nAn optional lossless source view is a record DAG, not the linear instruction\nimage. Its nodes are keyed by `[source-eid name-stack]`; decode re-merges\noccurrences by source eid. It preserves every `:yin/*` attribute, including\n`:yin/tail?` on every node, `:yin/macro-name`, exact scalar and name spelling,\none source eid for each occurrence, and all semantic references. It normalizes\nonly emitter-local\ntempids and `t`/`m`; non-`:yin` attributes are retained in a diagnostic side\narea unless the owner declares them irrelevant. Row order is normalized only\ninside the restricted input domain: one fact per entity and attribute and\nexactly one `:yin/root` marker. A batch accepted by named fallback rules\noutside that domain is a diagnostic for this encoding, not silently reordered.\nThis equality is up to a consistent entity-id renaming. It is not consumed by\nB2 or B5. Whether to retain this optional view, and therefore whether its\ninverse test is commissioned, is an owner decision; B0 does not depend on it.\n\nThe executable VM promises equivalence with the named semantic VM only after\nnormalization. Results, effects, streams, cursors, stores, terminal and\nblocked outcomes, and errors must agree. Error comparison uses the message;\nex-data is compared after recursively applying the value normalizer to every\nex-data value. Closures compare as `{:type :closure :arity n}` only;\ncontinuation and parked values compare by type only; stream and cursor\nreferences compare by identity; host handles and telemetry are excluded.\nBehavioural application tests cover closure contents. This normalizer is\ndefined and tested in B0, rather than assumed to be an existing repository\nutility.\n\nThe free environment is the VM's initial environment value, fixed for one VM\ninstance. It is not the mutable callee `:env` register left by an earlier\nnamed `run`. The named VM's environment leak is a separate defect and is not\nsilently included in this contract. B5 uses fresh VM instances for ordinary\nparity. Park/resume fixtures that cross program boundaries are restricted to\nfree names supplied by the initial environment or store until the named-VM\nleak is fixed; otherwise B5 records a named-path refusal.\n\nThe following are not promised to be identical: binder spelling in the\nprojected fingerprint, instruction program counters, temporary ids, allocation\nidentity, stack-trace text, source-map formatting, or interchangeability of\ncontinuations between the two VMs. The optional source view keeps enough\ninformation for decode and debugging; the executable image does not.\n\nThe VM obeys the datom.world invariants: state is explicit data, execution is\nan interpreter above streams, callbacks are never retained, effects are\nstream-visible, and the existing AST, projection, lease, waitset, and named VM\ncontracts are not changed.\n\n### Benefit and exit criterion\n\nThe primary benefit is network-shareable, alpha-invariant, content-addressed\nexecutable code over `dao.stream`. Existing named code is already content\naddressed by `yin.vm.content`; this adds binder-alpha-invariant image identity\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
